findings.finding:
My current finding is that every shifty α corresponds exactly to a polynomial  

\[
A(x)=\pm x^{a}\prod_{t\in T}\Phi_{2^{r+1}t}(x),
\]

where  

* \(a\ge0\) and \(a+\deg A\le8\);  
* \(r\ge0\) fixes the 2‑adic exponent (all cyclotomic factors have order \(2^{r+1}t\) with \(t\) odd);  
* \(T\) is any subset of the odd divisors of some odd integer \(o\) (the odd part of the shift‑parameter \(d\));  

and \(\Phi_{m}(x)\) denotes the \(m\)‑th cyclotomic polynomial.  This parametrisation follows because a divisor of \(1+x^{d}\) must be a product of those \(\Phi_{m}\) with \(m\mid2d\) but \(m\nmid d\), which forces all \(m\) to have the same 2‑adic valuation \(r+1\).  Consequently the set of all shifty functions is in bijection with the triples \((a,r,T)\) satisfying \(a+2^{r}\sum_{t\in T}\varphi(t)\le8\) (the sign gives a factor 2).  This corrects the earlier claim that any divisor of any \(1+x^{d}\) works; the 2‑adic condition is essential.
